{"name":"Arcadia","hex":"#00ac8d","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Arcadia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A regional unit of Greece in the central and southeastern Peloponnese. Tripoli is the capital and main city with a population over 47,000.","A mountainous region of Ancient Greece, named for Greek mythological hero Arcas; legendary home of the rustic god of nature, Pan."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An ideal region of rural and idyllic contentment; a pastoral utopia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Arcadia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Arcadia (2012 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Arcadia is a 2012 American drama film written and directed by Olivia Silver, produced by A Poisson Rouge Pictures and DViant Films. It won the Crystal Bears, Generation Kplus at 2012 Berlinale and the Grand Prize at the Cine Junior Festival."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/56/Arcadia_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arcadia_(2012_film)","wikidata":"Q12101183"},{"title":"Arcadia (2024 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Arcadia is a 2024 fantasy drama film directed by Yorgos Zois. Starring Angeliki Papoulia and Vangelis Mourikis, it tells the story of neurologist Katerina and Yannis, a former doctor, as they head off to a deserted seaside resort. Yannis has been called to identify the victim of a tragic accident at the hospital of the small town."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4e/Arcadia_2024_film_poster.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arcadia_(2024_film)","wikidata":"Q124467040"},{"title":"Arcadia (utopia)","blocks":[{"text":"Arcadia refers to a vision of the pastoral and harmony with nature. The term is derived from the Greek province of the same name which dates to antiquity; the province's mountainous topography and sparse population of pastoralists later caused the word Arcadia to develop into a poetic byword for an idyllic vision of unspoiled wilderness."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Cole_Thomas_The_Course_of_Empire_The_Arcadian_or_Pastoral_State_1836.jpg/330px-Cole_Thomas_The_Course_of_Empire_The_Arcadian_or_Pastoral_State_1836.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arcadia_(utopia)","wikidata":"Q168786"},{"title":"Arcadia (play)","blocks":[{"text":"Arcadia is a 1993 stage play written by English playwright Tom Stoppard, which explores the relationship between past and present, order and disorder, certainty and uncertainty. It has been praised by many critics as the finest play from \"one of the most significant contemporary playwrights\" in the English language. In 2006, the Royal Institution of Great Britain named it one of the best science-related works ever written."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/06/180px-Arcadia_book.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arcadia_(play)","wikidata":"Q672288"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg/500px-2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg/1280px-2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg/500px-2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/%E2%80%9CAfternoon_at_Arcadia%E2%80%9D_